// @check
// @error-at 14:6 undeclared identifier 'toString' — `toString` is not a builtin any more, it is `std:fmt`'s: add `import { toString } from "std:fmt"`
// @error-at 15:6 undeclared identifier 'toStr' — `toStr` was renamed to `toString`: add `import { toString } from "std:fmt"`
// @error-at 17:8 member access '.toString' on non-object i32 — `toString` is not a builtin any more, it is `std:fmt`'s: add `import { toString } from "std:fmt"`
// @error-at 18:8 member access '.toStr' on non-object i32 — `toStr` was renamed to `toString`: add `import { toString } from "std:fmt"`
// BOTH RETIRED SPELLINGS NAME THEIR REPLACEMENT (2026-09-01 — DECISIONS.md).
// `toString` was an ambient compiler builtin and `toStr` was `std:fmt`'s export
// name; the ruling retired the builtin and gave the export its name, so code
// written the day before stops resolving on either spelling. std has no
// deprecation story, so the compiler pays with a targeted refusal rather than a
// generic "undeclared identifier" — the `then`-removal precedent (#2094).
//
// Both POSITIONS carry it: the free-call spelling that the builtin served, and
// the member spelling that a migrating reader will reach for first.
print(toString(42))
print(toStr(42))
const n = 42
print(n.toString())
print(n.toStr())
